\nTOOL RESULT: exit code 0\ntotal 36\ndrwxrwxr-x  5 agent agent 4096\ndrwxr-xr-x 10 agent agent 4096\ndrwxr-xr-x  8 agent agent 4096\ndrwxrwxr-x  2 agent agent 4096\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent 9276\ndrwxr-xr-x 10 agent agent 4096\ndrwxr-xr-x 8 agent root 4096\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 root 268 Dec  4 2024 ./maelstrom\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent 9276 Nov 22 17:00 /usr/local/bin/maelstrom\n\n\nTOOL RESULT: exit code 0\noring: 24\n25 | `cas` requests may include `create_if_not_exists: true`, which\n26 | causes `cas` to create missing keys, rather than returning a key-not-found\n27 | error. This is particularly helpful for lazy initialization of state.\n29 | ## seq-kv\n30 | 31: A sequentially consistent key-value store. Just like lin-kv, but with relaxed\n32 | consistency.\n34 | 35: All operations appear to take place in a total order. Each client\n36 | observes a strictly monotonic order of operations. However, clients may\n37 | interact with past states of the key-value store, provided that interaction\n38 | does not violate these ordering constraints.\n39 | 40: This is *more* than simply stale reads: update operations may interact with\n41 | past states, so long as doing so would not violate the total-order constraints.\n42 | 43: For example, the following non-concurrent history is legal:\n44 | 45: 1. `n1` writes x = 1\n46 | 47: 2. `n2` compare-and-sets x from 1 to 2\n48 | 49: 3. `n1` writes x = 1\n50 | 51: 4. `n2` reads x = 2\n52 | 53: This is legal because `n1`'s second write can be re-ordered to the past without\n54 | 55: violating the per-process ordering constraint, and retaining identical\n56 | 57: semantics.\n58 | 59: 1. `n1` writes x = 1\n60 | 61: 2. `n1` writes x = 1\n62 | 63: 3. `n2` compare-and-sets x from 1 to 2\n64 | 65: 4. `n2` reads x = 2\n66 | 67: ## lww-kv\n68 | 69: An intentionally pathological last-write-wins key-value store. Simulates n\n70 | 71: (default: 5) independent nodes, each of which responds to KV requests\n71 | 72: independently. Each write is assigned a roughly synchronized timestamp. Nodes\n72 | 73: periodically gossip their values and merge them together, preferring higher\n73 | 74: timestamps. The API is identical to seq-kv and lin-kv.\n74 | 75: ## lin-tso\n76 | 76: A linearizable timestamp oracle, which produces a stream of monotonically\n77 | 78: increasing integers, one for each request. This is a key component in some distributed transaction algorithms--notably, Google's Percolator.\n79 | 79 | maelstrom-rename\n80 | 80 | `maelstrom` is not a registry but a package.\n81 | 81 | \n\nTOOL RESULT: exit code 0\n## Workload: Lin-kv \n...\n## Workload: Pn-counter \n... (pn-counter: add with delta, read; eventually consistent counter)\n## Workload: G-counter \n133: An eventually-consistent grow-only counter, which supports increments.\n135: Validates that the final read on each node has a value which is the sum of\n137: all known (or possible) increments.\n139-143: RPC Add!\n\n\nTOOL RESULT: exit code 0\nmaelstrom/workload/pn_counter$fn__20862.class\nmaelstrom/workload/pn_counter$workload.class\nmaelstrom/workload/pn_counter$fn__20921$read__20922.class\nmaelstrom/workload/pn_counter$checker$reify__20880$fn__20901.class\nmaelstrom/workload/pn_counter$acceptable__GT_vecs.class\nmaelstrom/workload/pn_counter__init.class\nmaelstrom/workload/g_counter$fn__20908.class\nmaelstrom/workload/g_counter$workload.class\nmaelstrom/workload/g_counter$loading__6812__auto____20847.class\nmaelstrom/workload/g_counter$fn__20921$read__20922.class\nmaelstrom/workload/pn_counter$acceptable_range.class\n... (list)\n\nTOOL RESULT: exit code 0\n==== maelstrom/workload/g_counter.clj\n(ns maelstrom.workload.g-counter\n  \"An eventually-consistent grow-only counter, which supports increments.\n  Validates that the final read on each node has a value which is the sum of\n  all known (or possible) increments.\n\n  See also: pn-counter, which is identical, but allows decrements.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [read])\n  (:require [jepsen.generator :as