Happy to. Here's how I'd approach it.\n\n**The shape of the problem.** Four nodes, a grow-only counter, partitions, and a final read on every node that must equal the sum of all acknowledged adds. The traps are (a) double counting, (b) losing an acknowledged add, and (c) a final read that's stale.\n\n**My design: per-node authoritative counters plus gossip; no shared writes.**\n\nEach node `n` owns exactly one number: `counts[n]`, the total visitors it has ever acknowledged. Only `n` ever writes it, and it only increases. Since increments are attributed to whichever node received the `count` request, no visitor can be counted twice — the sum over the four per-node numbers is exactly the multiset of accepted adds.\n\n- `count` handler: under a mutex, `counts[me] += visitors`; then reply `counted`; then fire a gossip round (asynchronously).\n- `gossip` message between nodes: `{\"type\":\"gossip\",\"counts\":{...}}`. Receiver merges per-key with `max`. That's a join-semilattice, so merge is commutative/associative/idempotent — duplicated, delayed and reordered gossip is harmless.\n- `total` handler: reply with the sum of my current merged map.\n- A background ticker re-broadcasts the full map to the other three nodes every ~50ms, so information lost to a partition is repaired within a tick or two of the heal.\n\n**Why gossip and not the KV store.** `seq-kv` is a shared mutable space and its documented semantics are scary: update operations can be re-ordered into the past, so two nodes writing (or even one node's older write vs. a newer write interleaved with a CAS) can lose an update. Per-node keys with only-increasing values mostly dodges that, but it still leaves the stale-read question at final-read time, and it adds a dependency that may itself be partitioned. Gossip has none of those ambiguities: the authoritative state lives in the node that accepted the add, and `max`-merge cannot go backwards. I'd use `seq-kv` only if testing showed the settle window was too tight — and even then, as a belt-and-braces `max` with local state, never as the sole source of truth.\n\n**The one risk.** Convergence speed at the end. The final read happens after the heal, and I haven't confirmed how much settle time Maelstrom gives — I was mid-way through reading `pn_counter.clj` (its checker and final generator) when I stopped. If there's a recovery sleep of a second or more, a 50ms gossip tick is plenty. If the final reads start immediately, a node could be short by the last few adds it hadn't heard about. Mitigations if needed: gossip immediately on every `count` (already planned), shorten the tick, and/or have `total` do a synchronous round-trip to peers (`RPC` with a short timeout) before answering, so a read also pulls fresh state rather than waiting for the next tick. That synchronous pull would make even an immediate post-heal read converge in one RPC round.\n\nSo: implement the gossip design, test it 6 times with the exact grading command, and only add peer-pull-on-read or `seq-kv` if a run fails. I'm ready to write the code when you say go.\n\nThe user's current request, verbatim:\n# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Visitor tally\n\nWrite a node for the four turnstiles of a museum, which together keep a running tally of\nvisitors. The tally only ever grows. Clients send each node two kinds of request.\n\n`count` reports that some visitors came in through this turnstile, a non-negative integer of them:\n\n    {\"type\": \"count\", \"msg_id\": 1, \"visitors\": 4}\n    {\"type\": \"counted\", \"in_reply_to\": 1}\n\n`total` asks for the tally so far, over all four turnstiles:\n\n    {\"type\": \"total\", \"msg_id\": 2}\n    {\"type\": \"total_is\", \"in_reply_to\": 2, \"visitors_so_far\": 118}\n\nThe nodes can share a key/value store that Maelstrom runs as a service named `seq-kv`. It is\nsequentially consistent: all nodes see its writes in one order, but a read may be a little out\nof date. In Go, `maelstrom.NewSeqKV(node)` gives you `Read`, `ReadInt`, `Write` and\n`CompareAndSwap`, each taking a `context.Context`. Nodes may also message each other in any\nformat you choose.\n\nFrom time to time the network splits the nodes into groups, and a message sent across a split\nis dropped silently. While that lasts, a `total` may leave out visitors counted elsewhere. At the\nend the network is healed and given time to settle, and then every node's `total` must equal the\nsum of every acknowledged `count`. A `count` that was never answered may be included or not, but\nno visitor may be counted twice.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 45 minutes and 150 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 6 times with:\n\n    MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN=/work/node MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"delta\": \"visitors\", \"value\": \"visitors_so_far\"}, \"types\": {\"add\": \"count\", \"add_ok\": \"counted\", \"read\": \"total\", \"read_ok\": \"total_is\"}}' maelstrom test --workload g-counter --bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename --nemesis partition --node-count 4 --rate 100 --time-limit 20\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n\nMaelstrom itself uses other names for these messages. `maelstrom-rename` stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node, so test with the command above as it is.\n", "usage": {"input_tokens": 10995, "output_tokens": 7090}}}
